{% doc %}
  Emits the `ItemList` JSON-LD describing the products on the current page of a
  collection grid.

  Rendered from `sections/main-collection.liquid`, inside its `{% paginate %}`
  block because it needs `paginate.current_offset`. Deliberately NOT rendered
  from `snippets/product-grid.liquid`: that snippet is shared with
  `sections/search-results.liquid`, and an `ItemList` on `/search` would assert
  a stable list for a query-dependent result set. Also not added to
  `templates/collection.json`, which Shopify generates and the theme editor can
  overwrite.

  A standalone `ItemList`, not a `CollectionPage` with `mainEntity`. Google does
  not consume `CollectionPage`, and it would want an `isPartOf` back to the
  `WebSite` `@id`, which puts an entity relationship on a non-homepage page.
  That is the neighbourhood of the homepage-only rule the rest of the
  structured data in this theme is built around.

  `ListItem`s carry a `url` and nothing else, which is Google's summary-page
  pattern. Full `Product` nodes here would duplicate the offers and prices
  Shopify's `structured_data` filter already emits on each product page, and
  would breach the standing rule that Product markup is never hand-authored.

  Three properties are deliberately absent:

  - `itemListOrder`. Asserting an order without checking the collection's actual
    sort is simply untrue for a `manual`, `best-selling`, or `created-descending`
    collection, and it is semantically valid JSON so no validator catches it.
  - `numberOfItems`. It must equal the `itemListElement` count, and the obvious
    source (`paginate.items`, the collection total) does not equal it on any page
    of a multi-page collection.
  - `item`. See the `url`-only note above.

  `position` is absolute (`forloop.index | plus: paginate.current_offset`), not
  page-relative. Page 2 restarting at position 1 would assert that a different
  product is the first item in the same list.

  Paginated views are NOT suppressed: Shopify canonicalises `?page=N` to itself,
  so each page is its own indexable URL and a list naming that page's slice with
  absolute positions is accurate. Infinite scroll does not change that; the
  JSON-LD describes the HTML that was served.

  Filtered and re-sorted views ARE suppressed. A filtered collection URL
  canonicalises back to the base collection, so a list emitted there describes
  one page under a URL pointing at another. A non-default sort changes the
  ordering the positions assert. An empty list is suppressed too, so a
  suppressed case emits no `<script>` tag at all rather than an empty array.

  `product.url` is used rather than a hand-built `/products/` path because it
  carries the locale and market path prefix. Inside a plain
  `{% for product in collection.products %}` loop it returns `/products/handle`,
  not the collection-scoped form, because scoping requires an explicit
  `| within:`.

  @param {object} collection - The collection being rendered.
  @param {object} paginate - The `paginate` object wrapping the grid. Supplies
    `current_offset` so positions stay absolute across pages.

  @example
    {% render 'structured-data-collection-list', collection: collection, paginate: paginate %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign list_is_scoped = false

  # A price filter with no selected values still reports `active_values.size`
  # as 0, so it needs its own check rather than riding on the loop above it.
  for filter in collection.filters
    if filter.active_values.size > 0
      assign list_is_scoped = true
      break
    endif
    if filter.type == 'price_range'
      if filter.min_value.value != blank or filter.max_value.value != blank
        assign list_is_scoped = true
        break
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  if collection.sort_by != blank and collection.sort_by != collection.default_sort_by
    assign list_is_scoped = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if list_is_scoped == false and collection.products.size > 0 -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "ItemList",
      "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
      "itemListElement": [
        {%- for product in collection.products -%}
          {%- unless forloop.first -%},{%- endunless -%}
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": {{ forloop.index | plus: paginate.current_offset }},
            "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }}
          }
        {%- endfor -%}
      ]
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
